{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>ואברכה מברכיך וגו׳</b> This blessing corresponds to what He concealed from him — the land — and did not inform him before his departure. And this trial is great: that he should leave his land for the field without knowing where he would turn, yet he did not harbor any second thought. Therefore He promised him that he need not fear anything, for He Himself would bless, etc., and in whatever place he would pitch his tent, there HaShem would ordain the blessing together with him. And through this, every place the sole of his foot would tread — his success and advancement would be with him." "<b>And there is</b> room to ask why He changed His expression regarding the matter of blessing? He said ״ואברכה מברכיך״ — He placed His blessing before their blessing, whereas with the curse He placed their curse before His curse. The reason is that the one who blesses — HaShem anticipates to bless him before he blesses Abraham, whereas the one who curses — HaShem does not anticipate to curse him. And the reason is that HaShem wishes to instill dread, lest a person treat Abraham's honor lightly. Were it not so, if HaShem were to anticipate to curse one who is destined to curse, it would not be felt, for that itself would be the cause, and people — both ordinary and prominent — would stumble in the matter. But in the case of blessing there is no stumbling-block. And if the intent is for the bestowal of good, behold all the families of the earth are blessed through it. Also, the reason for placing the blessing of those who bless first is so that their blessing to Abraham would be fulfilled." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to say — given what we examine: why did He mention those who bless in plural language, whereas those who curse He mentioned in singular?" "<b>Now,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what they said in tractate Mo'ed Katan (9a), that the rabbis said to the son of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: ״תזרע ולא תחצד״ etc., and they intended by this a blessing. And even though they expressed their words in the language of a curse, everything follows the intention — thus they blessed him in the format of a curse. Corresponding to this, HaShem said ״ואברכה מברכיך״, and likewise ״ומקללך״ — there is a kind of curser who is actually blessing, and him too I will bless; and therefore He used singular language, for not every curser will He bless, but only the specific one who intends a blessing — whereas ״מברכיך״ — all of them He will bless. And the word ״מקללך״ is also drawn downward, when his curse will prove to be a curse — ״אאר״. You thus find that HaShem intends, regarding those who bless, to bless all of them, and those who curse are two specific cases: one case that is in fact a blessing, and the word is drawn upward, and one case that is an actual curse, and the word is drawn downward." "<b>Further,</b> it may wish on this path to say: <b>ואברכה מברכיך</b>, meaning — when you have, in one blessing, two, namely the curser who intends a blessing, as in the incident where they said ״תזרע״ and the father returned and blessed him, HaShem established a blessing for both of them — so that you should not say that the primary blesser is the first one and the second is merely explanatory, Scripture therefore states ״מברכיך״, for both of them will be blessed. And His saying <b>ומקללך</b> also has a connection upward, and it is along the lines of what they said in tractate Ta'anit (20a), and this is their language: ״A curse that Ahijah the Shilonite cursed them is better than a blessing that Balaam blessed them״ etc. And corresponding to such a curser, He said that he is included within the blessing, and therefore He alluded in singular language, for this specific case alone He intends to bless; whereas ״ומקללך״ — another specific case, one who is nothing but a curser — for this: ״אאר״." "<b>ונברכו בך וגו׳.</b> And this is the distinction between one who blesses you and one who neither blesses nor curses: the one who blesses — HaShem will bless him from His own mouth, in His glory, but the rest of the families of the earth will be blessed through the medium of Avraham."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:14.178580234Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}